// The smallest grammar there is, and the smallest step up from it, in one file so that
// the two are read against each other.
//
// `A` is one character: a room check and a comparison, folded into a single `if` because
// they fail the same way. `B` is four, which is a different shape entirely — one span
// comparison the JIT folds into a single 64-bit `cmp`, with the position of the character
// that did not fit worked out afterwards, on the branch that comparison already failed.
//
// Nothing else differs between them, so the diff between those two methods is exactly
// what the extra three characters cost.
//
// `C` and `D` are the same three literals in two orders, and the diff between their
// methods is what the order costs. `D` is written longest-first: each text is decided
// where it differs from the ones before it, nothing is written down to come back to,
// and the whole rule is silent and lowered. `C` puts `"http"` in front of `"https"`,
// so the shorter takes the position wherever the longer would have and the choice
// needs a way back — which is the arena, and the engine, and everything under it.
//
// Both accept the same input under `parse`, which reads to the end and comes back for
// the other alternative when the first leaves a character over. `find` is where the
// order changes the answer rather than the price, and is why §11 promises the
// alternatives are never reordered.
//
// `E` and `F` are the two orders again with something after them that can spend the
// character the longer alternative would have taken, so both need a way back and
// neither is lowered. They are here for the way back itself, and the two are not the
// same: in `E` the later alternative continues the earlier one, so the way back is
// written past what already matched and reads one character. In `F` the earlier is
// the longer, which is the direction the texts cannot settle on their own, and the
// general machinery compiles it — one state per alternative, each comparing its own
// text from the beginning.

A = "a"
B = "abcd"
C = "http" | "https" | "ftp"
D = "https" | "http" | "ftp"
E = ("http" | "https") & "s"
F = ("https" | "http") & "s"

parse A
parse B
parse C
parse D
parse E
parse F
